[{"input":"# Chapter 5 – Software and Machine Instructions\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\nAt the end of this chapter, students should be able to:\n- Distinguish between hardware and software.\n- Explain the different categories of software.\n- Understand how a program is translated into executable instructions.\n- Explain the concepts of source code, machine code, and assembly language.\n- Understand the roles of compilers and interpreters.\n- Read and reason about simple machine instructions.\n- Appreciate the relationship between programming languages and computer hardware.","output":"","result":"# Chapter 5 – Software and Machine Instructions\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\nAt the end of this chapter, students should be able to:\n- Distinguish between hardware and software.\n- Explain the different categories of software.\n- Understand how a program is translated into executable instructions.\n- Explain the concepts of source code, machine code, and assembly language.\n- Understand the roles of compilers and interpreters.\n- Read and reason about simple machine instructions.\n- Appreciate the relationship between programming languages and computer hardware.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"chapter\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3369-chapter-5-software-and-machine-instructions\">\n<h1>Chapter 5 – Software and Machine Instructions</h1>\n<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3369-learning-objectives\">\n<h1>Learning Objectives</h1>\n<p>At the end of this chapter, students should be able to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Distinguish between hardware and software.</li>\n<li>Explain the different categories of software.</li>\n<li>Understand how a program is translated into executable instructions.</li>\n<li>Explain the concepts of source code, machine code, and assembly language.</li>\n<li>Understand the roles of compilers and interpreters.</li>\n<li>Read and reason about simple machine instructions.</li>\n<li>Appreciate the relationship between programming languages and computer hardware.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Introduction\n\nIn Chapter 4, we studied the hardware of a computer:\n- CPU\n- Memory\n- Storage\n- Input and output devices\n\nWe learned that processors execute instructions.\n\nThis naturally leads to an important question:\n\n> Where do these instructions come from?\n\nThe answer is:\n\n```text\nSoftware\n```\n\nSoftware tells hardware what to do.\n\nWithout software, a computer would be little more than an expensive collection of electronic components.\n\nThis chapter explains how software is created and how it ultimately becomes instructions that the processor can execute.","output":"","result":"## Introduction\n\nIn Chapter 4, we studied the hardware of a computer:\n- CPU\n- Memory\n- Storage\n- Input and output devices\n\nWe learned that processors execute instructions.\n\nThis naturally leads to an important question:\n\n> Where do these instructions come from?\n\nThe answer is:\n\n```text\nSoftware\n```\n\nSoftware tells hardware what to do.\n\nWithout software, a computer would be little more than an expensive collection of electronic components.\n\nThis chapter explains how software is created and how it ultimately becomes instructions that the processor can execute.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3371-introduction\">\n<h1>Introduction</h1>\n<p>In Chapter 4, we studied the hardware of a computer:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>CPU</li>\n<li>Memory</li>\n<li>Storage</li>\n<li>Input and output devices</li>\n</ul>\n<p>We learned that processors execute instructions.</p>\n<p>This naturally leads to an important question:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Where do these instructions come from?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The answer is:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">Software</pre>\n<p>Software tells hardware what to do.</p>\n<p>Without software, a computer would be little more than an expensive collection of electronic components.</p>\n<p>This chapter explains how software is created and how it ultimately becomes instructions that the processor can execute.</p>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## What Is Software?\n\nSoftware is a collection of instructions that tell a computer how to perform tasks.\n\nExamples include:\n- Web browsers\n- Games\n- Mobile apps\n- Operating systems\n- Scientific software\n- Artificial intelligence applications\n\n### Hardware versus Software\n\n#### Hardware\n\nPhysical components:\n- CPU\n- RAM\n- SSD\n- Keyboard\n- Screen\n\n#### Software\n\nInstructions and programs:\n- Windows\n- Linux\n- Chrome\n- Spotify\n- Microsoft Word\n\n### An Analogy\n\nImagine a kitchen.\n\n#### Hardware\n- Oven\n- Refrigerator\n- Cooking utensils\n\n#### Software\n- The recipes\n\nThe same kitchen can produce very different meals depending on which recipe is followed.\n\nSimilarly, the same computer can perform very different tasks depending on the software being executed.","output":"","result":"## What Is Software?\n\nSoftware is a collection of instructions that tell a computer how to perform tasks.\n\nExamples include:\n- Web browsers\n- Games\n- Mobile apps\n- Operating systems\n- Scientific software\n- Artificial intelligence applications\n\n### Hardware versus Software\n\n#### Hardware\n\nPhysical components:\n- CPU\n- RAM\n- SSD\n- Keyboard\n- Screen\n\n#### Software\n\nInstructions and programs:\n- Windows\n- Linux\n- Chrome\n- Spotify\n- Microsoft Word\n\n### An Analogy\n\nImagine a kitchen.\n\n#### Hardware\n- Oven\n- Refrigerator\n- Cooking utensils\n\n#### Software\n- The recipes\n\nThe same kitchen can produce very different meals depending on which recipe is followed.\n\nSimilarly, the same computer can perform very different tasks depending on the software being executed.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3372-what-is-software\">\n<h1>What Is Software?</h1>\n<p>Software is a collection of instructions that tell a computer how to perform tasks.</p>\n<p>Examples include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Web browsers</li>\n<li>Games</li>\n<li>Mobile apps</li>\n<li>Operating systems</li>\n<li>Scientific software</li>\n<li>Artificial intelligence applications</li>\n</ul>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3372-hardware-versus-software\">\n<h1>Hardware versus Software</h1>\n<section data-type=\"sect3\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3372-hardware\">\n<h1>Hardware</h1>\n<p>Physical components:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>CPU</li>\n<li>RAM</li>\n<li>SSD</li>\n<li>Keyboard</li>\n<li>Screen</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect3\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3372-software\">\n<h1>Software</h1>\n<p>Instructions and programs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Windows</li>\n<li>Linux</li>\n<li>Chrome</li>\n<li>Spotify</li>\n<li>Microsoft Word</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3372-an-analogy\">\n<h1>An Analogy</h1>\n<p>Imagine a kitchen.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect3\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3372-hardware-2\">\n<h1>Hardware</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>Oven</li>\n<li>Refrigerator</li>\n<li>Cooking utensils</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect3\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3372-software-2\">\n<h1>Software</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>The recipes</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The same kitchen can produce very different meals depending on which recipe is followed.</p>\n<p>Similarly, the same computer can perform very different tasks depending on the software being executed.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Types of Software\n\n### System Software\n\nSystem software manages the computer itself.\n\nExamples:\n- Windows\n- Linux\n- macOS\n- Android\n\nThe most important system software is the operating system.\n\n### Application Software\n\nApplication software solves specific problems for users.\n\nExamples:\n- Microsoft Word\n- Excel\n- Google Chrome\n- Adobe Photoshop\n\n### Libraries\n\nLibraries are collections of reusable software components.\n\nExamples:\n- Graphics libraries\n- Networking libraries\n- Mathematical libraries\n\n### Why Libraries Matter\n\nLibraries provide reusable building blocks.","output":"","result":"## Types of Software\n\n### System Software\n\nSystem software manages the computer itself.\n\nExamples:\n- Windows\n- Linux\n- macOS\n- Android\n\nThe most important system software is the operating system.\n\n### Application Software\n\nApplication software solves specific problems for users.\n\nExamples:\n- Microsoft Word\n- Excel\n- Google Chrome\n- Adobe Photoshop\n\n### Libraries\n\nLibraries are collections of reusable software components.\n\nExamples:\n- Graphics libraries\n- Networking libraries\n- Mathematical libraries\n\n### Why Libraries Matter\n\nLibraries provide reusable building blocks.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3373-types-of-software\">\n<h1>Types of Software</h1>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3373-system-software\">\n<h1>System Software</h1>\n<p>System software manages the computer itself.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Windows</li>\n<li>Linux</li>\n<li>macOS</li>\n<li>Android</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The most important system software is the operating system.</p>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3373-application-software\">\n<h1>Application Software</h1>\n<p>Application software solves specific problems for users.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Microsoft Word</li>\n<li>Excel</li>\n<li>Google Chrome</li>\n<li>Adobe Photoshop</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3373-libraries\">\n<h1>Libraries</h1>\n<p>Libraries are collections of reusable software components.</p>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Graphics libraries</li>\n<li>Networking libraries</li>\n<li>Mathematical libraries</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3373-why-libraries-matter\">\n<h1>Why Libraries Matter</h1>\n<p>Libraries provide reusable building blocks.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Programs and Algorithms\n\nAn algorithm is a description of a procedure.\n\n### Example Algorithm: Making Tea\n\n1. Boil water.\n2. Place tea bag in cup.\n3. Pour water.\n4. Wait.\n5. Remove tea bag.\n\nA program is an implementation of an algorithm in a programming language.\n\n```text\nAlgorithm\n↓\nProgram\n↓\nComputer Execution\n```","output":"","result":"## Programs and Algorithms\n\nAn algorithm is a description of a procedure.\n\n### Example Algorithm: Making Tea\n\n1. Boil water.\n2. Place tea bag in cup.\n3. Pour water.\n4. Wait.\n5. Remove tea bag.\n\nA program is an implementation of an algorithm in a programming language.\n\n```text\nAlgorithm\n↓\nProgram\n↓\nComputer Execution\n```","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3374-programs-and-algorithms\">\n<h1>Programs and Algorithms</h1>\n<p>An algorithm is a description of a procedure.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3374-example-algorithm-making-tea\">\n<h1>Example Algorithm: Making Tea</h1>\n<ol>\n<li>Boil water.</li>\n<li>Place tea bag in cup.</li>\n<li>Pour water.</li>\n<li>Wait.</li>\n<li>Remove tea bag.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A program is an implementation of an algorithm in a programming language.</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">Algorithm\n↓\nProgram\n↓\nComputer Execution</pre>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Programming Languages\n\nProgramming languages bridge the gap between humans and computers.\n\n### High-Level Languages\n\nExamples:\n- Python\n- Java\n- C#\n- JavaScript\n- C++\n\n### Example\n\n```text\ntotal = price + tax\n```\n\n### Why Not Program in Binary?\n\n```text\n01101010 11000101\n```\n\nHumans find such instructions difficult to read and write.","output":"","result":"## Programming Languages\n\nProgramming languages bridge the gap between humans and computers.\n\n### High-Level Languages\n\nExamples:\n- Python\n- Java\n- C#\n- JavaScript\n- C++\n\n### Example\n\n```text\ntotal = price + tax\n```\n\n### Why Not Program in Binary?\n\n```text\n01101010 11000101\n```\n\nHumans find such instructions difficult to read and write.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3375-programming-languages\">\n<h1>Programming Languages</h1>\n<p>Programming languages bridge the gap between humans and computers.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3375-highlevel-languages\">\n<h1>High-Level Languages</h1>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Python</li>\n<li>Java</li>\n<li>C#</li>\n<li>JavaScript</li>\n<li>C++</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3375-example\">\n<h1>Example</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">total = price + tax</pre>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3375-why-not-program-in-binary\">\n<h1>Why Not Program in Binary?</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">01101010 11000101</pre>\n<p>Humans find such instructions difficult to read and write.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Source Code\n\nSource code is the program written by a programmer.\n\n### Example\n\n```javascript\nlet age = 20;\n\nif (age >= 18) {\n    console.log(\"Adult\");\n}\n```\n\nProcessors understand only machine instructions, so source code must be translated.","output":"","result":"## Source Code\n\nSource code is the program written by a programmer.\n\n### Example\n\n```javascript\nlet age = 20;\n\nif (age >= 18) {\n    console.log(\"Adult\");\n}\n```\n\nProcessors understand only machine instructions, so source code must be translated.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3376-source-code\">\n<h1>Source Code</h1>\n<p>Source code is the program written by a programmer.</p>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3376-example\">\n<h1>Example</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"javascript\">let age = 20;\n\nif (age &gt;= 18) {\n    console.log(\"Adult\");\n}</pre>\n<p>Processors understand only machine instructions, so source code must be translated.</p>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Machine Code\n\nMachine code is the language understood by the processor.\n\n```text\n10110011\n00010010\n11000110\n...\n```\n\n### Characteristics\n\n- Extremely precise\n- Extremely fast\n- Difficult for humans to read\n\n### CPU Execution\n\n```text\nFetch\nDecode\nExecute\n```","output":"","result":"## Machine Code\n\nMachine code is the language understood by the processor.\n\n```text\n10110011\n00010010\n11000110\n...\n```\n\n### Characteristics\n\n- Extremely precise\n- Extremely fast\n- Difficult for humans to read\n\n### CPU Execution\n\n```text\nFetch\nDecode\nExecute\n```","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3377-machine-code\">\n<h1>Machine Code</h1>\n<p>Machine code is the language understood by the processor.</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">10110011\n00010010\n11000110\n...</pre>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3377-characteristics\">\n<h1>Characteristics</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>Extremely precise</li>\n<li>Extremely fast</li>\n<li>Difficult for humans to read</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3377-cpu-execution\">\n<h1>CPU Execution</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">Fetch\nDecode\nExecute</pre>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Assembly Language\n\nAssembly language is a human-readable representation of machine code.\n\n```text\nLOAD A\nADD B\nSTORE C\n```\n\n### Advantages\n\n- Easier to read\n- Easier to write\n- Easier to debug\n\n### Disadvantages\n\n- Detailed\n- Hardware-specific\n- Time-consuming","output":"","result":"## Assembly Language\n\nAssembly language is a human-readable representation of machine code.\n\n```text\nLOAD A\nADD B\nSTORE C\n```\n\n### Advantages\n\n- Easier to read\n- Easier to write\n- Easier to debug\n\n### Disadvantages\n\n- Detailed\n- Hardware-specific\n- Time-consuming","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3378-assembly-language\">\n<h1>Assembly Language</h1>\n<p>Assembly language is a human-readable representation of machine code.</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">LOAD A\nADD B\nSTORE C</pre>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3378-advantages\">\n<h1>Advantages</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>Easier to read</li>\n<li>Easier to write</li>\n<li>Easier to debug</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3378-disadvantages\">\n<h1>Disadvantages</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>Detailed</li>\n<li>Hardware-specific</li>\n<li>Time-consuming</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## From High-Level Language to Machine Code\n\n```text\nSource Code\n↓\nCompiler or Interpreter\n↓\nMachine Code\n↓\nCPU Execution\n```\n\nExample:\n\n```text\ntotal = 2 + 3\n```\n\nbecomes:\n\n```text\nLOAD 2\nLOAD 3\nADD\nSTORE RESULT\n```","output":"","result":"## From High-Level Language to Machine Code\n\n```text\nSource Code\n↓\nCompiler or Interpreter\n↓\nMachine Code\n↓\nCPU Execution\n```\n\nExample:\n\n```text\ntotal = 2 + 3\n```\n\nbecomes:\n\n```text\nLOAD 2\nLOAD 3\nADD\nSTORE RESULT\n```","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3379-from-highlevel-language-to-machine-code\">\n<h1>From High-Level Language to Machine Code</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">Source Code\n↓\nCompiler or Interpreter\n↓\nMachine Code\n↓\nCPU Execution</pre>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">total = 2 + 3</pre>\n<p>becomes:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">LOAD 2\nLOAD 3\nADD\nSTORE RESULT</pre>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Compiled Languages\n\nExamples:\n- C\n- C++\n- Rust\n- Go\n\n### Compilation\n\n```text\nSource Code\n↓\nCompiler\n↓\nExecutable Program\n```\n\nAdvantages:\n- Fast execution\n- Optimization opportunities\n\nDisadvantages:\n- Compilation takes time\n- Platform-specific executables","output":"","result":"## Compiled Languages\n\nExamples:\n- C\n- C++\n- Rust\n- Go\n\n### Compilation\n\n```text\nSource Code\n↓\nCompiler\n↓\nExecutable Program\n```\n\nAdvantages:\n- Fast execution\n- Optimization opportunities\n\nDisadvantages:\n- Compilation takes time\n- Platform-specific executables","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3380-compiled-languages\">\n<h1>Compiled Languages</h1>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>C</li>\n<li>C++</li>\n<li>Rust</li>\n<li>Go</li>\n</ul>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3380-compilation\">\n<h1>Compilation</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">Source Code\n↓\nCompiler\n↓\nExecutable Program</pre>\n<p>Advantages:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fast execution</li>\n<li>Optimization opportunities</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Disadvantages:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Compilation takes time</li>\n<li>Platform-specific executables</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Interpreted Languages\n\nExamples:\n- Python\n- JavaScript\n\n### Interpretation\n\n```text\nSource Code\n↓\nInterpreter\n↓\nExecution\n```\n\nAdvantages:\n- Easier experimentation\n- Faster development\n\nDisadvantages:\n- Slower execution","output":"","result":"## Interpreted Languages\n\nExamples:\n- Python\n- JavaScript\n\n### Interpretation\n\n```text\nSource Code\n↓\nInterpreter\n↓\nExecution\n```\n\nAdvantages:\n- Easier experimentation\n- Faster development\n\nDisadvantages:\n- Slower execution","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3381-interpreted-languages\">\n<h1>Interpreted Languages</h1>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Python</li>\n<li>JavaScript</li>\n</ul>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3381-interpretation\">\n<h1>Interpretation</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">Source Code\n↓\nInterpreter\n↓\nExecution</pre>\n<p>Advantages:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Easier experimentation</li>\n<li>Faster development</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Disadvantages:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Slower execution</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Virtual Machines\n\nExample: Java\n\n```text\nSource Code\n↓\nIntermediate Code\n↓\nVirtual Machine\n↓\nExecution\n```\n\nBenefits:\n- Greater portability\n- Same program on different operating systems","output":"","result":"## Virtual Machines\n\nExample: Java\n\n```text\nSource Code\n↓\nIntermediate Code\n↓\nVirtual Machine\n↓\nExecution\n```\n\nBenefits:\n- Greater portability\n- Same program on different operating systems","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3382-virtual-machines\">\n<h1>Virtual Machines</h1>\n<p>Example: Java</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">Source Code\n↓\nIntermediate Code\n↓\nVirtual Machine\n↓\nExecution</pre>\n<p>Benefits:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Greater portability</li>\n<li>Same program on different operating systems</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Understanding Machine Instructions\n\n### Data Movement\n\n```text\nLOAD\nSTORE\nMOVE\n```\n\n### Arithmetic Operations\n\n```text\nADD\nSUBTRACT\nMULTIPLY\n```\n\n### Comparisons\n\n```text\nCOMPARE\n```\n\n### Control Instructions\n\n```text\nJUMP\n```","output":"","result":"## Understanding Machine Instructions\n\n### Data Movement\n\n```text\nLOAD\nSTORE\nMOVE\n```\n\n### Arithmetic Operations\n\n```text\nADD\nSUBTRACT\nMULTIPLY\n```\n\n### Comparisons\n\n```text\nCOMPARE\n```\n\n### Control Instructions\n\n```text\nJUMP\n```","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3383-understanding-machine-instructions\">\n<h1>Understanding Machine Instructions</h1>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3383-data-movement\">\n<h1>Data Movement</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">LOAD\nSTORE\nMOVE</pre>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3383-arithmetic-operations\">\n<h1>Arithmetic Operations</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">ADD\nSUBTRACT\nMULTIPLY</pre>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3383-comparisons\">\n<h1>Comparisons</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">COMPARE</pre>\n</section>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3383-control-instructions\">\n<h1>Control Instructions</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">JUMP</pre>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Example: Adding Two Numbers\n\nGoal:\n\n```text\n5 + 3\n```\n\nProcessor instructions:\n\n```text\nLOAD 5\nLOAD 3\nADD\nSTORE RESULT\n```\n\nResult:\n\n```text\nRESULT = 8\n```","output":"","result":"## Example: Adding Two Numbers\n\nGoal:\n\n```text\n5 + 3\n```\n\nProcessor instructions:\n\n```text\nLOAD 5\nLOAD 3\nADD\nSTORE RESULT\n```\n\nResult:\n\n```text\nRESULT = 8\n```","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3384-example-adding-two-numbers\">\n<h1>Example: Adding Two Numbers</h1>\n<p>Goal:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">5 + 3</pre>\n<p>Processor instructions:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">LOAD 5\nLOAD 3\nADD\nSTORE RESULT</pre>\n<p>Result:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">RESULT = 8</pre>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Conditional Execution\n\n```text\nIF temperature > 30\nTHEN turn on fan\n```\n\nImplementation:\n\n```text\nCOMPARE temperature, 30\nJUMP_IF_GREATER turn_on_fan\n```","output":"","result":"## Conditional Execution\n\n```text\nIF temperature > 30\nTHEN turn on fan\n```\n\nImplementation:\n\n```text\nCOMPARE temperature, 30\nJUMP_IF_GREATER turn_on_fan\n```","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3385-conditional-execution\">\n<h1>Conditional Execution</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">IF temperature &gt; 30\nTHEN turn on fan</pre>\n<p>Implementation:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">COMPARE temperature, 30\nJUMP_IF_GREATER turn_on_fan</pre>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Repetition and Loops\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nCount from 1 to 10\n```\n\nConceptual loop:\n\n```text\nPRINT NUMBER\nINCREMENT NUMBER\nJUMP TO START\n```","output":"","result":"## Repetition and Loops\n\nExample:\n\n```text\nCount from 1 to 10\n```\n\nConceptual loop:\n\n```text\nPRINT NUMBER\nINCREMENT NUMBER\nJUMP TO START\n```","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3386-repetition-and-loops\">\n<h1>Repetition and Loops</h1>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">Count from 1 to 10</pre>\n<p>Conceptual loop:</p>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">PRINT NUMBER\nINCREMENT NUMBER\nJUMP TO START</pre>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Why Understanding Machine Code Matters\n\nUnderstanding machine instructions helps explain:\n- How software controls hardware\n- Why programming languages exist\n- Why computers execute instructions sequentially\n- How programming concepts map onto processor operations\n\n### Mental Model\n\n```text\nProblem\n↓\nAlgorithm\n↓\nSource Code\n↓\nMachine Code\n↓\nProcessor\n```","output":"","result":"## Why Understanding Machine Code Matters\n\nUnderstanding machine instructions helps explain:\n- How software controls hardware\n- Why programming languages exist\n- Why computers execute instructions sequentially\n- How programming concepts map onto processor operations\n\n### Mental Model\n\n```text\nProblem\n↓\nAlgorithm\n↓\nSource Code\n↓\nMachine Code\n↓\nProcessor\n```","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3387-why-understanding-machine-code-matters\">\n<h1>Why Understanding Machine Code Matters</h1>\n<p>Understanding machine instructions helps explain:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>How software controls hardware</li>\n<li>Why programming languages exist</li>\n<li>Why computers execute instructions sequentially</li>\n<li>How programming concepts map onto processor operations</li>\n</ul>\n<section data-type=\"sect2\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3387-mental-model\">\n<h1>Mental Model</h1>\n<pre data-type=\"program-listing\" data-code-language=\"text\">Problem\n↓\nAlgorithm\n↓\nSource Code\n↓\nMachine Code\n↓\nProcessor</pre>\n</section>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Summary\n\n- Software consists of instructions executed by hardware.\n- Software can be divided into system software, applications, and libraries.\n- Programs implement algorithms.\n- High-level languages make programming easier for humans.\n- Source code must be translated into machine code.\n- Machine code is the language executed by processors.\n- Assembly language is a readable representation of machine instructions.\n- Compilers translate programs before execution.\n- Interpreters translate programs during execution.\n- Processors execute instructions such as loading, storing, calculating, comparing, and jumping.\n- Programming languages ultimately control hardware through machine instructions.","output":"","result":"## Summary\n\n- Software consists of instructions executed by hardware.\n- Software can be divided into system software, applications, and libraries.\n- Programs implement algorithms.\n- High-level languages make programming easier for humans.\n- Source code must be translated into machine code.\n- Machine code is the language executed by processors.\n- Assembly language is a readable representation of machine instructions.\n- Compilers translate programs before execution.\n- Interpreters translate programs during execution.\n- Processors execute instructions such as loading, storing, calculating, comparing, and jumping.\n- Programming languages ultimately control hardware through machine instructions.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3388-summary\">\n<h1>Summary</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>Software consists of instructions executed by hardware.</li>\n<li>Software can be divided into system software, applications, and libraries.</li>\n<li>Programs implement algorithms.</li>\n<li>High-level languages make programming easier for humans.</li>\n<li>Source code must be translated into machine code.</li>\n<li>Machine code is the language executed by processors.</li>\n<li>Assembly language is a readable representation of machine instructions.</li>\n<li>Compilers translate programs before execution.</li>\n<li>Interpreters translate programs during execution.</li>\n<li>Processors execute instructions such as loading, storing, calculating, comparing, and jumping.</li>\n<li>Programming languages ultimately control hardware through machine instructions.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Key Terms\n\n| Term | Definition |\n|---|---|\n| Software | Collection of instructions executed by hardware |\n| System Software | Software that manages computer resources and hardware |\n| Application Software | Software designed to perform specific user tasks |\n| Library | Reusable collection of software components |\n| Algorithm | Step-by-step procedure for solving a problem |\n| Program | Implementation of an algorithm in a programming language |\n| Programming Language | Formal language used to write software |\n| Source Code | Human-readable program written by a programmer |\n| Machine Code | Binary instructions executed directly by a CPU |\n| Assembly Language | Human-readable representation of machine instructions |\n| Compiler | Program that translates source code into executable code |\n| Interpreter | Program that translates and executes source code |\n| Virtual Machine | Software layer that executes intermediate code |\n| Instruction Set | Collection of machine instructions supported by a processor |\n| Executable Program | Program ready to run on a computer |","output":"","result":"## Key Terms\n\n| Term | Definition |\n|---|---|\n| Software | Collection of instructions executed by hardware |\n| System Software | Software that manages computer resources and hardware |\n| Application Software | Software designed to perform specific user tasks |\n| Library | Reusable collection of software components |\n| Algorithm | Step-by-step procedure for solving a problem |\n| Program | Implementation of an algorithm in a programming language |\n| Programming Language | Formal language used to write software |\n| Source Code | Human-readable program written by a programmer |\n| Machine Code | Binary instructions executed directly by a CPU |\n| Assembly Language | Human-readable representation of machine instructions |\n| Compiler | Program that translates source code into executable code |\n| Interpreter | Program that translates and executes source code |\n| Virtual Machine | Software layer that executes intermediate code |\n| Instruction Set | Collection of machine instructions supported by a processor |\n| Executable Program | Program ready to run on a computer |","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3389-key-terms\">\n<h1>Key Terms</h1>\n<figure data-type=\"table\">\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term</th>\n<th>Definition</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Software</td>\n<td>Collection of instructions executed by hardware</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>System Software</td>\n<td>Software that manages computer resources and hardware</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Application Software</td>\n<td>Software designed to perform specific user tasks</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Library</td>\n<td>Reusable collection of software components</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Algorithm</td>\n<td>Step-by-step procedure for solving a problem</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Program</td>\n<td>Implementation of an algorithm in a programming language</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Programming Language</td>\n<td>Formal language used to write software</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Source Code</td>\n<td>Human-readable program written by a programmer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Machine Code</td>\n<td>Binary instructions executed directly by a CPU</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Assembly Language</td>\n<td>Human-readable representation of machine instructions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Compiler</td>\n<td>Program that translates source code into executable code</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Interpreter</td>\n<td>Program that translates and executes source code</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Virtual Machine</td>\n<td>Software layer that executes intermediate code</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Instruction Set</td>\n<td>Collection of machine instructions supported by a processor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Executable Program</td>\n<td>Program ready to run on a computer</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</figure>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Review Questions\n\n- What is the difference between hardware and software?\n- What is the role of system software?\n- Give three examples of application software.\n- What is an algorithm?\n- What is the relationship between an algorithm and a program?\n- Why do programmers use high-level languages instead of machine code?\n- What is source code?\n- Why must source code be translated before execution?\n- What is the difference between machine code and assembly language?\n- What is the role of a compiler?\n- What is the role of an interpreter?\n- What are the advantages and disadvantages of compiled languages?\n- What are the advantages and disadvantages of interpreted languages?\n- What is a virtual machine?\n- Describe the path from a programming problem to CPU execution.\n- What types of instructions can a processor execute?\n- How can conditional execution be implemented using machine instructions?\n- Why is understanding machine code useful for computer scientists and engineers?","output":"","result":"## Review Questions\n\n- What is the difference between hardware and software?\n- What is the role of system software?\n- Give three examples of application software.\n- What is an algorithm?\n- What is the relationship between an algorithm and a program?\n- Why do programmers use high-level languages instead of machine code?\n- What is source code?\n- Why must source code be translated before execution?\n- What is the difference between machine code and assembly language?\n- What is the role of a compiler?\n- What is the role of an interpreter?\n- What are the advantages and disadvantages of compiled languages?\n- What are the advantages and disadvantages of interpreted languages?\n- What is a virtual machine?\n- Describe the path from a programming problem to CPU execution.\n- What types of instructions can a processor execute?\n- How can conditional execution be implemented using machine instructions?\n- Why is understanding machine code useful for computer scientists and engineers?","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3390-review-questions\">\n<h1>Review Questions</h1>\n<ul>\n<li>What is the difference between hardware and software?</li>\n<li>What is the role of system software?</li>\n<li>Give three examples of application software.</li>\n<li>What is an algorithm?</li>\n<li>What is the relationship between an algorithm and a program?</li>\n<li>Why do programmers use high-level languages instead of machine code?</li>\n<li>What is source code?</li>\n<li>Why must source code be translated before execution?</li>\n<li>What is the difference between machine code and assembly language?</li>\n<li>What is the role of a compiler?</li>\n<li>What is the role of an interpreter?</li>\n<li>What are the advantages and disadvantages of compiled languages?</li>\n<li>What are the advantages and disadvantages of interpreted languages?</li>\n<li>What is a virtual machine?</li>\n<li>Describe the path from a programming problem to CPU execution.</li>\n<li>What types of instructions can a processor execute?</li>\n<li>How can conditional execution be implemented using machine instructions?</li>\n<li>Why is understanding machine code useful for computer scientists and engineers?</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Transition to Lecture 6\n\nWe now understand how software is transformed into machine instructions that hardware can execute.\n\nHowever, modern software systems rarely consist of a single program operating in isolation. Most applications must interact with users, files, networks, and other programs while sharing computer resources efficiently.\n\nIn Lecture 6, we will study **operating systems**, the software layer that manages hardware resources, coordinates program execution, controls memory and storage, and provides the environment in which applications run.","output":"","result":"## Transition to Lecture 6\n\nWe now understand how software is transformed into machine instructions that hardware can execute.\n\nHowever, modern software systems rarely consist of a single program operating in isolation. Most applications must interact with users, files, networks, and other programs while sharing computer resources efficiently.\n\nIn Lecture 6, we will study **operating systems**, the software layer that manages hardware resources, coordinates program execution, controls memory and storage, and provides the environment in which applications run.","resultHTML":"<section data-type=\"sect1\" id=\"nb-SC612_Lecture05-c3391-transition-to-lecture-6\">\n<h1>Transition to Lecture 6</h1>\n<p>We now understand how software is transformed into machine instructions that hardware can execute.</p>\n<p>However, modern software systems rarely consist of a single program operating in isolation. Most applications must interact with users, files, networks, and other programs while sharing computer resources efficiently.</p>\n<p>In Lecture 6, we will study <strong>operating systems</strong>, the software layer that manages hardware resources, coordinates program execution, controls memory and storage, and provides the environment in which applications run.</p>\n</section>\n","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null}]